      <section class="section">
        <p>Propellant blend formulation is the technical practice of mixing liquefied hydrocarbon gases—predominantly propane, n-butane, and isobutane—to engineer a precise vapor pressure inside an aerosol container. This formulation regulates spray atomization, container pressure consistency, and discharge characteristics throughout the product's lifetime, ensuring that the active ingredients are delivered in a uniform pattern. Understanding how these gases blend and exert pressure is critical for engineers because it directly affects package safety, formulation stability, and spray quality. By adjusting the ratios of high-pressure and low-pressure components, formulators can design customized delivery profiles for personal care, household, paint, and industrial specialty products. For a detailed review of alternative propellant choices like DME and fluorocarbons, refer to our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-propellants-explained-lpg-vs-dme-vs-hfc-vs-n2-vs-co2" target="_top">aerosol propellants explained</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <section id="section-1" class="section">
        <h2>Thermodynamic Principles of Vapor Pressure in LPG Blends</h2>
        <p>The thermodynamic behavior of a liquefied petroleum gas (LPG) propellant inside an aerosol can is governed by liquid-vapor phase equilibrium. Within the closed system of a metal container, the liquefied gas exists in a state of dynamic equilibrium where the rate of evaporation of molecules from the liquid surface equals the rate of condensation of vapor molecules back into the liquid phase. The pressure exerted by the gas phase under these equilibrium conditions is defined as the vapor pressure, which is solely a function of temperature and chemical composition. As long as some liquid propellant remains in the container, this pressure will remain constant at a given temperature, regardless of the volume of liquid present. This unique property of liquefied gas propellants differentiates them from compressed gases (such as nitrogen or carbon dioxide), where pressure drops linearly as the product is discharged. For a detailed comparison of compressed and liquefied systems, check out our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/propellant-influence-on-aerosol-performance" target="_top">propellant influence on aerosol performance</a>.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Dynamic Equilibrium:</strong> Evaporation and condensation occur at equal rates, maintaining constant headspace pressure.</li>
          <li><strong>Temperature Dependence:</strong> Vapor pressure increases exponentially with temperature, following the Clausius-Clapeyron relation.</li>
          <li><strong>Independent of Volume:</strong> Liquid phase depletion does not drop pressure, ensuring consistent delivery until exhaustion.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

