        <h2>Chemical Composition and Physical Properties of Binary LPG Propellants</h2>
        <p>The primary hydrocarbon propellants used in aerosol packaging are propane, n-butane, and isobutane. Each of these components possesses distinct physical properties, such as molecular weight, boiling point, density, and pure vapor pressure, which are documented in technical databases like <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubChem (NIH)</a>. Propane, with a boiling point of -42.1°C, is a high-pressure agent that provides the primary force in formulations requiring high atomization energy. Conversely, n-butane has a boiling point of -0.5°C and exerts a very low vapor pressure, making it useful as a diluent and pressure reducer. Isobutane, an isomer of n-butane, has a boiling point of -11.7°C and provides an intermediate pressure, serving as the workhorse propellant in many cosmetic and personal care aerosols. The selection of these materials is based on their physical compatibility, cost, environmental profile, and chemical stability in aqueous or solvent-based concentrates.</p>
        
