      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="section-3" class="section">
        <h2>Mathematical Formulation: Raoult's Law and Dalton's Law Calculations</h2>
        <p>Predicting the final vapor pressure of a propellant blend formulation requires applying fundamental thermodynamic laws to the mixture. In an ideal solution, the partial vapor pressure of each component (P<sub>i</sub>) is calculated using Raoult's Law, which states that P<sub>i</sub> = X<sub>i</sub> · P<sub>i</sub>°, where X<sub>i</sub> is the mole fraction of the component in the liquid phase and P<sub>i</sub>° is the vapor pressure of the pure component at that temperature. According to Dalton's Law of partial pressures, the total vapor pressure of the mixture (P<sub>total</sub>) is the sum of the partial pressures of all individual components: P<sub>total</sub> = P<sub>1</sub> + P<sub>2</sub> + ... + P<sub>n</sub>. Formulators use molecular weights and liquid densities to convert weight percentages into mole fractions, allowing them to calculate the exact pressure curve of binary and ternary mixtures. Thermodynamic data for these calculations can be cross-referenced with the <a href="https://webbook.nist.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST WebBook</a> database.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Mole Fraction (X<sub>i</sub>):</strong> Calculated by dividing the moles of a component by the total moles of all components in the liquid phase.</li>
          <li><strong>Raoult's Law (P<sub>i</sub> = X<sub>i</sub> · P<sub>i</sub>°):</strong> Assumes ideal chemical behavior, which is a highly accurate approximation for non-polar liquefied hydrocarbons.</li>
          <li><strong>Dalton's Law (P<sub>total</sub> = Σ P<sub>i</sub>):</strong> Adds the calculated partial pressures to predict the overall container headspace pressure.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section id="section-4" class="section">
        <h2>Vapor Pressure Control and Phase Behavior in Aerosol Cans</h2>
        <p>In a packaged aerosol product, the propellant blend is not isolated; it is contact-blended with the liquid concentrate, which often contains solvents, surfactants, active ingredients, and water. When the propellant dissolves in the concentrate, it forms a homogeneous single-phase solution or a heterogeneous emulsion, modifying the overall vapor pressure of the system. For instance, polar solvents like ethanol exhibit high affinity for hydrocarbon propellants, forming a solution that lowers the propellant's effective vapor pressure below the calculated ideal value. In contrast, in water-based systems where hydrocarbons are insoluble, the propellant forms a separate liquid layer floating on top of the aqueous phase, and the headspace pressure remains close to the pure propellant pressure. Understanding these phase interactions is essential for maintaining spray quality and avoiding valve clogging. For details on container materials and preventing leaks in such systems, read our article on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-can-safety-explained" target="_top">aerosol can safety explained</a>.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); background: #f0fdf4; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0; margin: 24px 0;">
          <h4 style="margin: 0 0 6px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--green);">Key Insight</h4>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">Solvent choice directly dictates the active vapor pressure in homogeneous aerosol systems. High-affinity organic solvents lower vapor pressure through molecular solvation, whereas immiscible aqueous concentrates allow the propellant to exert its full vapor pressure independently.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

