      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section id="section-5" class="section">
        <h2>Propellant Blend Formulation and Industrial Safety Standards</h2>
        <p>Because liquefied hydrocarbon propellants are highly flammable gases, industrial blending, storage, and filling processes are subject to rigorous safety engineering standards. Facilities handling these propellants must comply with safety codes such as the <a href="https://www.nfpa.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NFPA 58 (Liquefied Petroleum Gas Code)</a> and NFPA 30B (Code for the Manufacture and Storage of Aerosol Products). Equipment must be designed to withstand high operational pressures and prevent gas leaks that could form explosive mixtures in the air. Blending is typically carried out inline using mass flow meters or in pressurized vessels gunder nitrogen blankets to prevent oxygen contamination. All electrical hardware in the blending and gassing zones must be rated Class I, Division 1, Group D, utilizing explosion-proof enclosures or intrinsic safety barriers. In addition, high-capacity ventilation systems and continuous gas monitoring arrays are mandatory to automatically shut down gassing operations if gas concentration exceeds 20% of the Lower Explosive Limit (LEL).</p>
        
        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--amber); background: #fffbeb; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0; margin: 24px 0;">
          <h4 style="margin: 0 0 6px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--amber);">Rule of Thumb</h4>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">Always maintain continuous static grounding loops across all piping, blending vessels, and aerosol gassing heads. Hydrocarbon liquid flow generates static charges that can trigger ignition if discharge paths are not continuously grounded.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section id="section-6" class="section">
        <h2>Quality Control, Vapor Pressure Measurement and Leak Testing Protocols</h2>
        <p>Quality control in propellant blending ensures that the delivered raw materials meet the precise chemical composition and vapor pressure specifications. Aerosol manufacturers perform gas chromatography (GC) on incoming propellant shipments to verify the hydrocarbons' relative ratios and detect impurities, such as heavy oil residues or excessive moisture, which can damage valve gaskets. Once the aerosol can is filled, closed, and gassed, it undergoes continuous inline quality checks, including weight checks to confirm the correct propellant charge and water bath testing. According to dangerous goods transport regulations published by the <a href="https://unece.org/dangerous-goods" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UNECE (United Nations Economic Commission for Europe)</a>, every filled aerosol container must be tested in a hot water bath at 50°C (or an approved alternative method) to verify structural integrity and ensure it does not leak or burst under thermal stress.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Gas Chromatography:</strong> Quantifies butane, propane, and isobutane mole percentages to ensure raw material consistency.</li>
          <li><strong>Hot Water Bath Testing:</strong> Heats cans to 50°C to increase internal pressure, verifying crimp integrity and detecting leaks.</li>
          <li><strong>Vapor Pressure Gauging:</strong> Measures headspace pressure at standard reference temperatures to catch blending ratio errors.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ Section -->
